The window bundles SolidJS (MIT), three.js (MIT, https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js; the 3D view of
3D frames, with its orbit controls and wide lines) and the IBM Plex fonts (SIL Open Font Licence 1.1). The desktop
shell uses Tauri (MIT or Apache-2.0) and the Rust crates listed in Cargo.lock, each under MIT,
Apache-2.0, BSD, ISC, Zlib, MPL-2.0 or Unicode licences (checked by cargo deny, deny.toml). The sparse Cholesky solver is faer (MIT,
https://github.com/sarah-quinones/faer-rs), run on every core with rayon (MIT or Apache-2.0). Parts are meshed with spade (MIT or Apache-2.0,
https://github.com/Stoeoef/spade), constrained Delaunay triangulation and refinement. Shapes are combined
(union, subtract, intersect) with i_overlay and its i_float, i_shape, i_tree and i_key_sort crates (MIT or
Apache-2.0, https://github.com/iShape-Rust/iOverlay).

The materials library (crates/core/data/materials.json, built by tools/materials.py) is copied
from Datum Atlas (MIT, https://github.com/Edgar-Mendonca/Datum-Atlas), with each value's source
as Atlas gives it. Poisson's ratios are typical values for each material family (Atlas has none).

No GPL, LGPL or AGPL code is linked into or bundled with Datum Mesh. Open solvers and meshers
under those licences, planned for later releases, will only ever run as separate programs.

The family workflow, ribbon, File page, updater and printing are taken from Datum Plan, by way of
Datum Sim (both MIT, same author). The structural steel values come from EN 1993-1-1 §3.2 and
EN 10025-2; each material keeps its source in the model.
